TI  - Cross-sectional study to identify risk factors for impaired early childhood development in rural India
PY  - 2020
T2  - Building Children and Youth for the Future: Some International Aspects
SP  - 151
EP  - 172
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85111332119&partnerID=40&md5=b23db7fd5f8490bdb77d4c40491f9c94
AD  - Department of Paediatrics, IQ City Medical College, Durgapur, India
AB  - Healthy development in the early years provides building blocks for educational achievement, emotional and moral development. Enabling young children to achieve their full developmental potential is a requisite for sustainable development. Early childhood development (ECD) is affected by multiple adverse biological and socio-economic factors, exposure to which is commonly met with among the under-privileged. This study aims to assess the social and medical adversities faced by young children in the under-privileged rural community in India. A random sample survey was conducted in 523 households with children aged 0-6 years, in Eastern India, to identify the prevalent risk factors deterrent to optimum brain development. Adversities like poor living conditions, lack of stimulatory home environment, learning opportunities and responsive parental care were identified. As a cumulative effect of these factors children were failing to achieve their developmental potential. Maternal education was the single most significant factor having an impact on these adversities and hence on ECD. Conclusion: Adequate education and empowerment of primary care-givers, especially mothers, can help to improve ECD. © 2021 Nova Science Publishers, Inc.
