AD  - Department of Mental Health, Johns Hopkins Bloomberg School of Public Health, Baltimore, MD, United States
AB  - An estimated 200 million children under age 5 in low- and middle-income countries (LMIC) fail to reach their developmental potential. Advances in neuroscience show that exposure to poverty-related cumulative risk during early childhood may affect brain structure and compromise early development potentially by limiting opportunities for cognitive stimulation. Cognitive delays impair children’s potential to achieve academic success, which can lead to a cascade of adverse outcomes, including lower income potential and subsequent intergenerational transmission of poverty. Although there are evidence-based programs for promoting cognitive development among children living in poverty in low- and middle-income countries, because they have generally only been implemented by highly skilled providers, who are costly and limited in supply, the possibility of scaling up and disseminating such programs is limited. One such program is the Mediational Intervention for Sensitizing Caregivers (MISC), a program that uses specialized providers to train caregivers to be more attuned to their child’s needs. However, the financial costs and human resources needed to expand the reach of this program may be prohibitive in its current format, which includes individual treatment over 12-months by technical or university-educated providers. This review describes evidence for various mHealth innovation technologies that can significantly enhance the dissemination and implementation science for such early childhood developmental interventions such as MISC. © 2022 selection and editorial matter, Lochner Marais and Carla Sharp.
