TI  - Executive function in adult survivors of prematurity
PY  - 2014
T2  - Executive Functioning: Role in Early Learning Processes, Impairments in Neurological Disorders and Impact of Cognitive Behavior Therapy (CBT)
SP  - 83
EP  - 114
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84954147237&partnerID=40&md5=6697a78dc9c696222e422f3becb9822d
AD  - Brown Center for the Study of Children at Risk, Women and Infants Hospital of Rhode Island, Providence, RI, United States
AD  - College of Nursing, University of Rhode Island, Kingston, RI, United States
AD  - University of Chicago Comer Children's Hospital, Section of Developmental and Behavioral Pediatrics, United States
AD  - JP Kennedy Research Center on Intellectual and Developmental Disabilities, Chicago, IL, United States
AB  - The research evidence for executive function outcomes of prematurely born infants is mixed. Some report poor executive function outcomes in children aged preschool through late adolescence involving working memory, cognitive shifting or flexibility, problem solving, attention, and impulse control. Other studies document improvement from early deficits at adolescence. The heterogeneity of premature morbidities may partially account for these differences. Brain maturation in the third trimester likely plays a role as myelination of white matter, growth of gyri, sulci, synapses and dendritic aborization occurs. Also, ecological factors of risk and protection help to inform a comprehensive view of the effects of prematurity on young adult executive function. Early executive function is important because these skills underlie academic, social, and adaptive functioning. This chapter will: (1) Describe executive function at age 23 years in a well-characterized sample with a range of wide preterm neonatal morbidities and a full term comparison group. (2) Model ecological influences of risk and protection on young adult executive function. (3) Examine the impact of executive dysfunction on adult independent living, educational attainment, employment, and occupation status. This is a prospective, longitudinal cohort of 215 infants born preterm, classified by neonatal morbidity into 4 groups, and a full term group. Executive function was measured by the Behavior Rating Inventory of Executive Function-Adult (BRIEF-A) and the Delis-Kaplan Executive Function System (D-KEFS). Ecological factors include socioeconomic status (SES) and cumulative indexes of medical risk and protection from birth through adolescence. Confirmatory factor analysis generated these indexes with appropriate fit statistics. Analyses included ANOVA, hierarchical linear modeling and chi-square. There were no differences between the neonatal groups on the BRIEF-A. Ecological influences of SES, medical risk and protection explained executive function scores. Models supported independent and interaction effects. High medical risk predicted poor executive function. SES and protection predicted better executive function. Young adults with executive dysfunction were more likely to live with parents, not graduate from college, and work in unskilled jobs. This study takes a broader view of prematurity and examines ecological context at age 23 years. A comprehensive understanding of adult executive function is better explained by ecological factors, not prematurity alone. Executive dysfunction was associated with poorer educational, adaptive and occupational functions at age 23. Finally, consequences of executive dysfunction were significantly more adverse when coupled with prematurity and neonatal illness. This translates to diminished chances for living independently, attaining college degrees, and working in skilled occupations. © 2014 by Nova Science Publishers, Inc. All rights reserved.
