TI  - Poverty and low educational attainment are serious, inhibiting influences on early childhood development. What is the task for pediatric primary care?
ST  - Armut und bildungsferne als schwerwiegende, hemmende einflüsse auf die frühkindliche entwicklung: Welche aufgabe ergibt sich für die pädiatrische grundversorgung?
PY  - 2019
T2  - Padiatrische Praxis
VL  - 92
IS  - 1
SP  - 152
EP  - 170
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85091571829&partnerID=40&md5=6120f9fdfb282df615d57069b9138e1a
AD  - Deutsches Kinderbulletin, Oranienburg, Germany
AB  - In Germany, around 2.5 million children under the age of 15 grow up in low socioeconomic status (SES: low educational attainment and low income). Even in their first years of life, these children often fail to reach their full potential in cognitive, motor and social-emotional development. In later years, they have a much less favourable school-, work- and social prognosis compared to children from a parental home with higher SES. Early childhood development is determined by genetic factors, by an epigenetic modulation of gene expression, by a rapid brain development with its neuro- and synaptogenesis and by the extent of a stimulating home environment. A good stimulation of early childhood development has a demonstrable, lifelong effect. A key task of Primary Care Paediatricians (PCPs) is the early detection of families with low SES whose children grow up in unfavourable developmental conditions. PCPs will inform the families about help and support services (HSS) in the community. Ideally, to get in contact with the HSS, families should be guided by pilotage services. Stimulating children's development in the first years of life is best achieved in those HSS, where at the same time the mother / parents can be integrated and supported socially and educationally, f.e. in kindergartens of family centres. If appropriate HSS are lacking, PCPs often prescribe speech or occupational therapy, however these medical remedies sofar had little lasting effects. © 2019 Mediengruppe Oberfranken - Fachverlage GmbH & Co. KG. All rights reserved.
