AD  - Hosp. for Children and Adolescents, 00029 HUS, Helsinki, P.O. Box 280, Finland
AB  - Objective: To investigate the effect of antiepileptic drugs, especially carbamazepine and valproate, on intelligence in prenatally exposed children of mothers with epilepsy. Methods: Intelligence of 182 children of mothers with epilepsy (study group) and 141 control children was tested in a blinded setting at preschool or school age using Wechsler Preschool and Primary Scale of Intelligence-Revised or Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children-Revised. Data on maternal antiepileptic treatment and seizures during pregnancy were gathered prospectively. The study group represented approximately 50% of the children born to mothers with epilepsy in Uusimaa province during 1989 through 1994. One hundred seven children were exposed to antiepileptic monotherapy: 86 to carbamazepine and 13 to valproate. Thirty children were exposed to polytherapy: 23 combinations included carbamazepine, and 17 included valproate. The median maternal doses and blood levels during the second half of pregnancy were 600 mg and 26 μmol/L for carbamazepine and 950 mg and 300 μmol/L for valproate. Results: The mean verbal and nonverbal IQ scores in the children exposed in utero to carbamazepine monotherapy were 96 (95% CI, 93-100) and 103 (95% CI, 100-106). They did not differ from control subjects, whose mean verbal and nonverbal IQ scores were 95 (95% CI, 92-97) and 102 (95% CI, CI, 100-105). Significantly reduced verbal IQ scores were found in children exposed to valproate (mean, 82; 95% CI, 78-87) and to polytherapy (mean, 85; 95% CI, 80-90) compared with the other study group children and control subjects. Conclusions: Carbamazepine monotherapy with maternal serum levels within the reference range does not impair intelligence in prenatally exposed offspring. Exposures to polytherapy and to valproate during pregnancy were associated with significantly reduced verbal intelligence. The independent effects of valproate remain unconfirmed because the results were confounded by low maternal education and polytherapy.; Indications:Unspecified number of mothers with epilepsy (idiopathic generalized epilepsy, cryptogenic epilepsy, symptomatic partial epilepsy and Baltic-type progressive myoclonic epilepsy).; Patients:182 children of 143 mothers comprised the study group. 45 had no drug exposure, 107 were exposed to monotherapy, including 86 Tegretol, 13 valproate and 8 other anticonvulsants, and 30 were exposed to polytherapy, including 23 Tegretol and 17 valproate. Total number of children exposed to Tegretol: 109. Number of mothers receiving Tegretol not stated. Control group: 141 children of 121 mothers.; TypeofStudy:The objective of this population based study was to investigate whether prenatal exposure to antiepileptic drugs (AEDs), especially Tegretol and valproate, impairs intelligence in prenatally exposed children.; DosageDuration:50 to 1300 mg daily. Duration not stated.; ComparativeDrug:Valproate dosage was 6 mg to 44 mg daily, duration not stated.; Results:There were no significant differences in the IQ measurements between the study group and the control subjects. WPPSI-R (given to children aged <6 years) gave significantly higher scores than WISC-R (given to children aged ≥ 6 years); this was always accounted for in covariance analysis. The group of children exposed to monotherapy and the subgroup exposed to Tegretol monotherapy performed equally well as the nonexposed children and control subjects. There were no significant correlations between IQ scores in the children and maternal daily doses or serum levels of Tegretol. Children exposed to polytherapy had significantly lower verbal IQ but similar nonverbal IQ when compared with study group children exposed to monotherapy or to control subjects. The difference remained significant after maternal education and IQ test were accounted for in covariance analysis. The mean (SEM; number of children) IQ scores in the 30 children exposed to valproate either as monotherapy or in combination were 82.3 (2.2; 29) for verbal, 96.2 (2.9; 29) for nonverbal, and 87.9 (2.1; 30) for full-scale IQ. The difference in verbal IQ between children exposed to valproate and other study group children (mean, 94.8; SEM, 1.4) was significant (F = 9.2; p = 0.003, with maternal education and IQ test as covariates). This difference persisted when number of AEDs was added in the analysis as a covariate (F = 6.0; p = 0.02). When siblings were excluded and only the eldest child of each mother was included in the analysis, the mean (SEM) IQ scores of the remaining 21 children exposed to valproate were 83.6 (2.7) for verbal and 95.9 (3.0) for nonverbal. By regression analysis, there was a negative relationship between verbal IQ and valproate dose (p = 0.04). Maternal epilepsy type, active epilepsy, or exposure to maternal generalized tonic-clonic seizures during pregnancy was not associated with reduced intelligence scores (all p values 0.4 or higher, accounting for test and maternal education). Mental retardation was found in four study group children (2%) and three control subjects (2%). Two study children had no drug exposure; one of them had Down syndrome, and the other had no identified etiology. Two retarded study children had been exposed to drugs: one to 400 mg daily of Tegretol and the other to 1,200 mg daily of valproate. The mother of the latter child had been tested in childhood and showed subnormal intelligence. None were exposed to maternal seizures during gestation. One control child had triploid mosaicism. Two retarded control children had no identified etiology.; AdverseEffects:No children had intellectual impairment.; AuthorsConclusions:Carbamazepine monotherapy with maternal serum levels within the reference range does not impair intelligence in prenatally exposed offspring. Exposures to polytherapy and to valproate during pregnancy were associated with significantly reduced verbal intelligence. The independent effects of valproate remain unconfirmed because the results were confounded by low maternal education and polytherapy.; FreeText:The diagnosis of epilepsy in the mothers of the participating 189 children was evaluated by one of the neurologists or pediatric neurologists by reviewing data obtained from hospital charts on seizure phenomenology, electroencephalography (EEG), etiologic studies including neuroimaging, past and present medication including drug levels, and family history. Many mothers had been personally cared for by one of the neurologists of this study. One hundred forty-three mothers were confirmed to have epilepsy that was classified according to the proposal for revised classification of epilepsies and epileptic syndromes. In the study group, 137 (75%) children had been exposed to AEDs during the second half of pregnancy. Of them, 107 had been exposed to monotherapy: 86 to Tegretol, 13 to valproate, and 8 to other drugs. Thirty children had been exposed to polytherapy: 17 combinations included valproate and 23 included Tegretol (plus valproate, clonazepam, phenytoin and phenobaritone in 13, 6, 2 and 2 patients respectively). There was a tendency to generalized epilepsy being more common in the mothers of the children exposed to valproate (60%) than in other study group children (40%; p = 0.07). Two mothers started Tegretol only in the third trimester; all others were treated with the same drugs throughout pregnancy. Maternal serum levels of valproate, phenytoin, and phenobarbital were known for all exposed children, and maternal serum levels of Tegretol were known for all except one. In the analyses, the highest maternal daily dose and drug level measured during the second half of pregnancy was used. The neuropsychological examination was done in a single session lasting 1.5 to 2 hours. Verbal, nonverbal, and full-scale intelligence quotients (IQs) were estimated using six subtests (Information, Arithmetic, Similarities, Picture Completion, Block Design, and Animal Houses/Picture Arrangement) of Wechsler Preschool and Primary Scale of Intelligence-Revised (WPPSI-R) (children aged <6 years) or Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children-Revised (WISC-R) (aged ≥ 6 years). Mental deficiency was defined as verbal and nonverbal IQ <70. Results were analyzed using statistical analysis.
