TI  - Three-month nutritional supplementation among Indonesian infants and toddlers benefits memory function eight years later
PY  - 1996
T2  - FASEB Journal
VL  - 10
IS  - 3
SP  - A290
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-33749101904&partnerID=40&md5=7c93f2c44cf4e172693b95d2866d0223
AB  - Does short-term supplementary feeding during infancy and childhood have long-term effects? In 1986 334 children aged 6-60 months living on tea plantât ions in West Java took part in a 3-month randomized trial to test the effects of a dietary supplement providing 777897 J of energy; because the supplement was locally made, the nutrient density was typical of local foods {Husaini et al., AJCN 54:799). In 1994 we returned to the same area and enrolled 231 (125 supplemented, 106 control) of the original subjects to study the longterm effects of supplementation. We assessed the subjects on anthropometry, iron status, information processing, Peabody Picture Vocabulary test, word fluency, arithmetic, and socioeconomic status. There were nc treatment effects when all subjects were analyzed. However, when the analysis was limited to subjects who received treatment before 18 months (n=73), the supplemented children performed better than control children on the Sternberg test of working memory {decision time intercept, probe absent p=0.002, probe present p=.U53). After considering possible confounders, we conclude that the supplementation during infancy was responsible for this difference. This demonstrates that supplementation can have long-lasting effects on a specific domain, but only if the child receives it at the appropriate stage of development.
