TI  - Cécile and Oskar Vogt: The visionaries of modern neuroscience
PY  - 2002
T2  - Acta Neurochirurgica, Supplement
IS  - 80
SP  - VI
EP  - 129
DO  - 10.1007/978-3-7091-6141-8
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-67049167148&doi=10.1007%2F978-3-7091-6141-8&partnerID=40&md5=42d6ff8f8516c2b339555397437619e5
AB  - In consideration of the Vogts' scientific achievements and life struggles, there is no question that a special genetic endowment and an unusual variety of environmental influences played a substantial role in the evolution of their scientific ideas and accomplishments. Genetically, they both possessed brilliantly intelligent minds, marked by insatiable curiosity, which continuously prompted them to ask questions and to search for answers. This was associated with a strong intellectual independence, which allowed them to accept the beliefs and rules of society only after critical analysis, and to reject those dogmas which they felt were wrong or lacking in sufficient supportive evidence. Environmentally, from early childhood they were exposed to harsh conditions, which required them to fight hard for achieving aims based on their independent judgment. This led both of them, as mentioned, to have confrontations with religion during their school years, which resulted in disinheritance of Cécile by her rich aunt, and a temporary expulsion of Oskar from the gymnasium for spreading "atheistic" Darwinian ideas among his classmates aimed against the established religious dogmas that the earth with its living creatures was created by God within a week. Otherwise, the struggle for obtaining a proper education under insecure and strained financial circumstances made them acquainted early with the social inequalities associated with poverty, an acquaintance that was likely to direct their political sympathies for the rest of their life towards liberal-leftist causes. It can be similarly assumed that the hardships and struggles had influenced their minds to search for a comprehensive explanation of the true nature of man, which could be obtained only by independent scientific inquiry. They wanted to learn all about the human body and mind, and they rejected the notion of separateness of body and "soul". They concluded that a human being functions by virtue of his/her organs and that the brain is the organ in charge of all psychic activities. The Vogts' supposition, at which they arrived soon after their marriage, was that various brain functions and psychic phenomena are related to different brain structures. They were able to provide documentation for this idea by detailed cytoarchitectonic studies, followed by correlating some distinct cellular characteristics of the cortical and subcortical areas with functional responses evoked by electrical stimulation in these regions. They met each other halfway, compromising on many points, but still retained their basic positions on the subject. More essential and difficult was reaching the truce concerning their thorny relationship following Spatz's assuming the directorship of the Buch Institute. They both were anxious to get off their shoulders a heavy load of mutual bitterness and misunderstanding, and they were able to reach a permanent truce in their relationship. This was facilitated by Spatz admitting his feeling of guilt with regard to Oskar, who, perhaps, feeling the approach of the end of his life, was eager to clean his desk. Cécile and Oskar Vogt were a unique couple. It is very seldom that such an incredible synchronization of aims, dreams, mutual understanding and respect takes place. Their intellectual endowments supplemented each other, as Cécile's critical and practical French mind was essential for giving reality to Oskar's grand conceptual ideas and visions, pronounced with unshakable German faith and certainty. Both of them were of a rebellious nature and shared a revolutionary spirit, breaking and transcending the rigid, established rules impeding human progress, and they never wavered or compromised their convictions, - neither in the time of the Kaiser's monarchy, nor during the shaky Weimar Republic, nor in the terror stricken Third Reich or in the period of post-war chaos. Being agnostic and deeply affected by Darwinism, their beliefs were based on an exploration of the laws of nature, and science becoming their religion. In science, a synchrony of characters adds a great strength and ability to reach the top and Santiago Ramon y Cajal, in his book on the Rules and Councils concerning a Scientific Investigation [94], singled out four such couples: the Curies, Déjérines, Nageottes, and the Vogts. About the latter he wrote: "... y en fin, los esposos O. Vogt y Cecilie Vogt, del Instituto neurobiologico de Berlin, ocupados en la magna empresa de la cartografia parcelaria del cerebro humano, al modo de los astronomos que se pasan la vida absortos en la fotografia y catalogacion de estrellas y nebulosas." Indeed, like astronomers attempting to define the Universe from their observations on celestial formations, the Vogts, by defining the cytoarchitectonic constellations of neurons, were laying the foundation of the current explosive progress of knowledge about mechanisms regulating the function of the brain. If still alive, the Vogts would be happy to see many of their visionary ideas being confirmed and providing guide posts for the field of modern Neuroscience.
